and succession planning gaps","lead":"Workforce challenges in adult social care affect both service delivery and cost management. Succession planning gaps and an ageing workforce profile indicate potential future staffing costs and service continuity risks.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"Appendix 2 for Draft Statement of Accounts and Draft Annual Governance Statement for 2025/26","paper_url":"https://councillors.liverpool.gov.uk/documents/s328597/328597.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Procurement and contract management show persistent compliance failures with financial regulations","lead":"Non-compliance with financial regulations and contract standing orders creates legal, financial and reputational risk. Procurement failures can result in cost overruns, legal challenge and supplier disputes that affect service delivery.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"Appendix 2 for Draft Statement of Accounts and Draft Annual Governance Statement for 2025/26","paper_url":"https://councillors.liverpool.gov.uk/documents/s328597/328597.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Financial resilience persists as material governance risk across multiple governance reviews","lead":"Persistent financial resilience concerns directly affect the Council's ability to maintain sustainable financial planning and control. This indicates ongoing structural financial pressure requiring continuous management attention and close monitoring.","iso":"2026-09-17","paper_title":"Appendix 2 for Draft Statement of Accounts and Draft Annual Governance Statement for 2025/26","paper_url":"https://councillors.liverpool.gov.uk/documents/s328597/328597.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Appendix 2 for Draft Statement of Accounts and Draft Annual Governance Statement for 2025/26","paper_url":"https://councillors.liverpool.gov.uk/documents/s328597/328597.pdf","headline":"Adult Social Care workforce sustainability at risk due to ageing workforce and succession planning gaps","detail":"Adult Social Care workforce sustainability is identified as an important governance consideration. The Council specifically identifies risks around succession planning, mandatory training compliance and the need to maintain service resilience in the context of an ageing workforce profile. This is flagged as requiring ongoing governance management attention.","category":"people","matters":"Workforce challenges in adult social care affect both service delivery and cost management. Succession planning gaps and an ageing workforce profile indicate potential future staffing costs and service continuity risks.","figures":"None provided","quote":"In adult social care, workforce sustainability remains an important governance consideration, including succession planning, mandatory training compliance and the need to maintain resilience in the context of an ageing workforce profile.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Appendix 2 for Draft Statement of Accounts and Draft Annual Governance Statement for 2025/26","paper_url":"https://councillors.liverpool.gov.uk/documents/s328597/328597.pdf","headline":"Procurement and contract management show persistent compliance failures with financial regulations","detail":"The Council identifies continued need for improvement in procurement and contract management discipline. Assurance evidence indicates ongoing non-compliance with Contract Standing Orders and Financial Regulations. The statement repeatedly emphasizes the need to 'strengthen consistent compliance,' indicating this is not a newly-identified issue but an area of sustained concern.","category":"governance","matters":"Non-compliance with financial regulations and contract standing orders creates legal, financial and reputational risk. Procurement failures can result in cost overruns, legal challenge and supplier disputes that affect service delivery.","figures":"None provided","quote":"assurance evidence also reinforces the need for consistent compliance with procurement rules, contract standing orders and financial regulations across all services, given the potential legal, financial and reputational consequences of non-compliance","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Appendix 2 for Draft Statement of Accounts and Draft Annual Governance Statement for 2025/26","paper_url":"https://councillors.liverpool.gov.uk/documents/s328597/328597.pdf","headline":"Financial resilience persists as material governance risk across multiple governance reviews","detail":"The Council explicitly identifies financial resilience as a key underlying governance consideration driven by national funding pressures and demand-led service pressures. Financial resilience appears in the Corporate Risk Register as requiring particularly close attention. The statement concludes that financial resilience remains a material governance risk requiring sustained improvement, indicating an ongoing challenge not resolved during the year.","category":"governance","matters":"Persistent financial resilience concerns directly affect the Council's ability to maintain sustainable financial planning and control. This indicates ongoing structural financial pressure requiring continuous management attention and close monitoring.","figures":"No quantified figures provided; identified as area requiring 'particularly close attention' in corporate risk register","quote":"financial resilience continues to represent a key underlying governance consideration, reflecting ongoing national funding pressures, demand-led service pressures and the need to maintain sustainable financial planning and robust financial control arrangements","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Appendix 2 for Draft Statement of Accounts and Draft Annual Governance Statement for 2025/26","paper_url":"https://councillors.liverpool.gov.uk/documents/s328597/328597.pdf","headline":"Information Commissioner formal intervention following Freedom of Information governance failures","detail":"The Council's 2024/25 period identified governance failings in its performance responding to Freedom of Information requests. An Information Commissioner formal decision was issued on 1 September 2025, representing external regulatory intervention. The Council identifies information governance as an area requiring continued improvement.","category":"governance","matters":"Formal intervention by the Information Commissioner indicates control weaknesses in statutory compliance that were not resolved internally. This reflects governance environment gaps and requires third-party regulatory action.","figures":"Decision issued 1 September 2025","quote":"The 2024/25 reporting period highlighted governance failings in the Council's performance in responding to requests under the Freedom of Information Act 2000.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-17","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"Appendix 2 Companies Governance Risk Register","paper_url":"https://councillors.liverpool.gov.uk/documents/s328583/328583.pdf","headline":"Company failure could trigger emergency shareholder support requirement on council cash","detail":"The register identifies that financial or operational failure of a council company could require emergency shareholder support or create impairment of council assets and stranded liabilities. This is rated medium residual risk (8/10) but represents a direct call on council finances.","category":"capital","matters":"Emergency shareholder support would consume reserves or require additional borrowing, directly affecting the council's debt affordability and financial flexibility.","figures":"Inherent 15/25 (High); Resultant 8/10 (Medium)","quote":"Financial or operational failure causes financial loss, service disruption, impairment of Council assets, emergency shareholder support or other stranded liabilities","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-17"},"name":"Liverpool","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-17","since_iso":"2026-08-18","distress":false,"headline":"Adult Social Care workforce sustainability at risk due to ageing workforce and succession planning gaps","lead":"Workforce challenges in adult social care affect both service delivery and cost management. Succession planning gaps and an ageing workforce profile indicate potential future staffing costs and service continuity risks.","also":["24 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Audit Committee on 2026-09-17","n_findings":25,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Appendix 2 for Draft Statement of Accounts and Draft Annual Governance Statement for 2025/26","top_paper_url":"https://councillors.liverpool.gov.uk/documents/s328597/328597.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":116,"news_drafts_total":3}}